foundation.solution_text:
1. **Mark the stationary points of $f$ as $x$-intercepts of $y = f'(x)$**
$y = f'(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x = -1$, $x = 1$ and $x = 3$.
2. **Use the intervals where $f$ is increasing or decreasing to place $y = f'(x)$ above or below the $x$-axis**
$f$ falls, rises, falls, then rises, so $f'$ is negative, positive, negative, then positive. Three intercepts and this sign pattern make $f'$ a cubic.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, xmin=-2.2, xmax=4.2, ymin=-6.4, ymax=6.4, xtick={-2,-1,1,2,3,4}, ytick={-6,-4,-2,2,4,6}, samples=120, clip=false, width=9cm, height=8cm]
\addplot[smooth, thick, <->, domain=-1.52:3.52] {x*x*x - 3*x*x - x + 3} node[pos=0.97, left] {$y = f'(x)$};
\node[circle, fill, inner sep=1.5pt] at (axis cs:-1,0) {};
\node[circle, fill, inner sep=1.5pt] at (axis cs:1,0) {};
\node[circle, fill, inner sep=1.5pt] at (axis cs:3,0) {};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
3. **Mark the points of inflection of $f$ as turning points of $y = f'(x)$ and as $x$-intercepts of $y = f''(x)$**
$f'$ has a maximum a little to the left of the origin and a minimum a little past $x = 2$, so $y = f''(x)$ crosses the axis at those two values.
4. **Use the concavity of $f$ to place $y = f''(x)$ above or below the $x$-axis**
$f$ is concave up, then concave down between the two inflections, then concave up again, so $f''$ is positive, negative, positive. Differentiating a cubic gives a parabola.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, xmin=-1.7, xmax=3.7, ymin=-5.4, ymax=6.4, xtick={-1,1,2,3}, ytick={-4,-2,2,4,6}, samples=100, clip=false, width=9cm, height=8cm]
\addplot[smooth, thick, <->, domain=-0.82:2.82] {3*x*x - 6*x - 1} node[pos=0.97, right] {$y = f''(x)$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

