mastery.question_text:
The graph of $y = f(x)$ below has a horizontal point of inflection at $x = 1$ and a minimum turning point at $x = 3$, and has no other stationary points.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, xmin=-1.1, xmax=4.7, ymin=-2.8, ymax=4.6, xtick={-1,1,2,3,4}, ytick={-2,-1,1,2,3,4}, samples=150, clip=false, width=9cm, height=8cm]
\addplot[smooth, thick, <->, domain=-0.52:4.15] {0.25*(x-1)*(x-1)*(x-1)*(x-1) - 0.6667*(x-1)*(x-1)*(x-1)};
\node[circle, fill, inner sep=1.5pt] at (axis cs:1,0) {};
\node[circle, fill, inner sep=1.5pt] at (axis cs:3,-1.333) {};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Sketch $y = f'(x)$, then sketch $y = f''(x)$.

mastery.solution_text:
1. **Mark the stationary points of $f$ as $x$-intercepts of $y = f'(x)$**
$y = f'(x)$ meets the $x$-axis at $x = 1$ and at $x = 3$.
2. **Use the intervals where $f$ is increasing or decreasing to place $y = f'(x)$ above or below the $x$-axis**
$f$ is decreasing on both sides of $x = 1$, so $f'$ touches the axis there without crossing it.
$f$ changes from decreasing to increasing at $x = 3$, so $f'$ crosses the axis there.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, xmin=-0.6, xmax=4.4, ymin=-5.2, ymax=6.4, xtick={-1,1,2,3,4}, ytick={-4,-2,2,4,6}, samples=120, clip=false, width=9cm, height=8cm]
\addplot[smooth, thick, <->, domain=-0.22:3.77] {(x-1)*(x-1)*(x-3)} node[pos=0.97, right] {$y = f'(x)$};
\node[circle, fill, inner sep=1.5pt] at (axis cs:1,0) {};
\node[circle, fill, inner sep=1.5pt] at (axis cs:3,0) {};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
3. **Mark the points of inflection of $f$ as turning points of $y = f'(x)$ and as $x$-intercepts of $y = f''(x)$**
$f'$ has a maximum at $x = 1$ and a minimum a little past $x = 2$, so $y = f''(x)$ crosses the axis at those two values.
4. **Use the concavity of $f$ to place $y = f''(x)$ above or below the $x$-axis**
$f$ is concave up for $x < 1$, concave down between the two inflections, and concave up again beyond, so $f''$ is positive, negative, positive.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, xmin=-0.7, xmax=3.7, ymin=-2.4, ymax=6.4, xtick={1,2,3}, ytick={-2,2,4,6}, samples=100, clip=false, width=9cm, height=8cm]
\addplot[smooth, thick, <->, domain=0.103:3.23] {(x-1)*(3*x-7)} node[pos=0.97, left] {$y = f''(x)$};
\node[circle, fill, inner sep=1.5pt] at (axis cs:1,0) {};
\node[circle, fill, inner sep=1.5pt] at (axis cs:2.3333,0) {};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

